*** {{audio|en|En-us-often.ogg|a=US,cot-caught}}
* In the Early Modern English standard, the {{ic|/t/}} was pronounced; [[orthoepist]]s ({{w|Alexander Gill the Elder|Gil}}, {{w|John Hart (spelling reformer)|Hart}}, {{w|Robert Robinson (phonetician)|Robinson}} et al.) record it into the 17th century. But by that century, or possibly earlier (a spelling without ''t'' is attested in one text from about 1410),<!--MED--> some speakers had begun to pronounce the word without {{IPAchar|/t/}},<ref name="MWG">[https://www.merriam-webster.com/grammar/correct-pronunciation-of-often Merriam Webster "How to Pronounce 'Often': To \t\ or not to \t\?"]</ref><ref>{{R:en:Dobson:1957|II|403|968}}</ref> parallelling the development of words such as {{m|en|listen}} and {{m|en|thistle}}. In the 18th century, Johnston, Perry, and Spence give a pronunciation with {{IPAchar|/t/}},<ref>{{R:en:Johnston:1764|óftꬲn|172|ip=1}}</ref><ref>{{R:en:Perry:1775|ŏft`<i>e</i>n|257}}</ref> while {{w|Thomas Sheridan (actor)|Sheridan}} and {{w|John Walker (lexicographer)|Walker}} give a pronunciation without it;<ref>{{R:en:Sheridan:1780|often|650}}</ref><ref>{{R:CPD|Often|383|ip=1}}</ref> by the following century, the pronunciation with {{IPAchar|/t/}} was usually proscribed but is described as "frequent".<ref name="MWG"/><ref>{{R:en:Meredith:1872|Often|33}}</ref><ref name="OEDfrequent">{{R:OED}}</ref> Today, both pronunciations are common and accepted by most dictionaries,<ref name="MWG"/><ref name="OEDfrequent"/><ref>{{R:Cambridge}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:Longman}}</ref> though for more than a century now, users of each pronunciation have been liable to regard theirs as the more correct or formal pronunciation.<ref>William Grant, ''The Pronunciation of English in Scotland'' (1913), volume 1, page 27: ''t'' is omitted regularly in ''listen'', ''must n't'', and ''often'', ˈlɪsn, ˈmʌsnt, ˈɔfən. ˈɔftən is sometimes heard, especially in very dignified speech.</ref><ref>Anne Curzan, ''Says Who?: A Kinder, Funner Usage Guide for Everyone'' (2025), page 94: The sound /t/ is coming back into ''often'', and while some people notice it, the newer pronunciation is largely unstigmatized. I am an "offen" speaker, but many of the undergraduates I teach have a /t/ in ''often'', either as their only pronunciation or their more formal pronunciation of ''often''. [...] For some speakers, the pronunciation "offen" seems sloppy (in other words, the historical pronunciation is becoming stigmatized), because we're not articulating all the letters in the spelling; the pronunciation "often" (with the /t/) is more careful and formal."</ref><ref>Charles Harrington Elster, ''The Big Book of Beastly Mispronunciations'' (2005), pages 354-356: "John Walker (1791) [...said] "in often and soften the ''t'' is silent." "The sounding of the t," [said] H. W. Fowler in Modern English Usage (1926), "which as the OED says is 'not recognized by the dictionaries, is practised by two oddly consorted classes — the academic speakers who affect a more precise enunciation than their neighbours ... & the uneasy half-literates who like to prove that they can spell...." "The t in glisten is silent, even as it is in castle and often," says Frank H. Vizetelly (1929), editor of Funk & Wagnalls New Standard (1913), "yet one occasionally hears pedants and provincials pronounce them [GLIS-ten] and [AWF-ten]. No pronouncing dictionary with a reputation to lose ever sounds the ''t'' in these words." "You don't want a ''t'' in here any more than in soften," advises Alfred H. Holt (1937). Webster 2 (1934), which sanctions only AWF-in, notes that "the pronunciation [AWF-tin], until recently generally considered as more or less illiterate, is not uncommon among the educated in some sections, and is often used in singing." "Often should be pronounced oFF-un, not oFF-tun," declare Morris & Morris (1985), "though the latter pronunciation is often affected, especially by singers." According to Random House II (1987), OFTEN was pronounced with a t sound until the 17th century, when a pronunciation without the (t) came to predominate in the speech of the educated, in both North America and Great Britain, and the earlier pronunciation fell into disfavor. Common use of a spelling pronunciation has since restored the (t) for many speakers, and today (AWF-in] and (AWF-tin) exist side by side. Although it is still sometimes criticized, OFTEN with a (t) is now so widely heard from educated speakers that it has become fully standard once again. "Nowadays," says R. W. Burchfield (1996), the late OED lexicographer, "many standard speakers use both [AWF-in] and [AWF-tin], but the former pronunciation is the more common of the two." What is going on here? After two hundred years of censure, has the tin often scratched and clawed its way back into acceptability? I would caution those who might be consoled by the comments of Random House II and Burchfield to heed the admonitions of the past and avoid pronouncing the t. Current dictionaries, including Random House II, do not give priority to AWF-tin, Encarta (2001) doesn't recognize it, authorities with reputations to lose still frown on it, and it is much less common in educated speech and far more often disapproved of by cultivated speakers — particularly teachers of English, drama, and speech — than Random House II makes it appear. In 1932 the English lexicographer Henry Cecil Wyld called AWF-tin "vulgar" and "sham-refined," and today the bad odor of class-conscious affectation still clings to it as persistently as ever, as the comments of Garner (2003) make clear: “The educated pronunciation is [AWF-in], but the less-adept say [AWF-tin]. To put it another way, the silent-t pronunciation is U; the spoken-t pronunciation is distinctly non-U.""</ref> 
{{cln|en|spelling pronunciations}}
* The reason why pronunciations with {{IPAchar|/t/}} persisted in ''often'', but mostly disappeared in e.g.  {{m|en|listen}} and {{m|en|thistle}} is unclear; it may be due to the influence of {{m|en|oft}} (compare the 18th-century pronunciation of {{m|en|chasten}} with {{IPAchar|/t/}}), {{glossary|spelling pronunciation}}, or merely because the loss of {{IPAchar|/t/}} after {{IPAchar|/f/}} was less throughgoing than that after {{IPAchar|/s/}}.
